"""Original participatory-governance learning within verified decision scope."""


def build(c, m):
    return c(76, 'Participatory Governance and Public Consultation', 'Participatory Governance\nand Public\nConsultation',
        'Design and review a bounded fictional public consultation with meaningful influence, accessible methods and an accountable feedback record.', [
        m('Set honest purpose scope and influence', 'Explain what a consultation can influence and who retains decision authority.', [
            'Public consultation invites contributions to a defined decision or understanding; it does not automatically transfer every decision power to participants. Begin with an honest statement of purpose, the choices still open and the role that will decide. OECD guidance emphasises meaningful participation, suitable methods and a connection between contributions and decisions. In the fictional exercise, explain whether input concerns priorities, delivery design or a review of experience. A process advertised as co-decision would be misleading if participants can only comment on a choice already fixed by an authorised body. [S53]',
            'Define the boundary in plain language before inviting views. Identify what is outside the exercise, why it is outside and how a relevant question could reach an appropriate verified route. A consultation about service hours cannot promise to change a benefit entitlement or settle a personal complaint. This distinction protects the quality of the conversation as well as the accuracy of public information. Use invented issues and aggregate descriptions only; learners should not request real case files, identify complainants or imply that taking part guarantees a personal remedy. [S53]',
            'Participatory budgeting illustrates both the possibilities and limits of participation. A qualitative meta-synthesis of fifteen case-study articles discusses institutional context, unequal participation and elite influence. Its interpretive synthesis does not estimate an average causal effect or establish a universally superior democratic design. Use those findings to ask which choices are genuinely open in the fictional budget exercise and how the process might exclude perspectives. Do not turn a context-dependent observation or a debatable normative statement into a rule that every institution must use the same model. [R65]',
            'Write a short invitation that states the purpose, open choice, responsible decision role, participation options and promised feedback. Specify the resources required to honour that promise, including the ability to review contributions and explain a decision. If no meaningful choice is open, describe an information session honestly or reconsider whether consultation is appropriate. The quality of the exercise depends on an accurate relationship between invitation and influence, not on attendance alone or a claim that a participatory label guarantees public trust. [S53]'],
            'A fictional council advertises a public vote on all service funding, although the supplied mandate leaves only opening-hour preferences open for consultation. Personal benefit appeals are also directed to the same meeting.',
            'Rewrite the invitation in five sentences, stating the open choice, decision role, out-of-scope issues, accessible contribution options and feedback commitment.',
            'Describe consultation on hours honestly. Explain the verified route needed for personal appeals without deciding them or promising powers the meeting does not hold.', ['S53', 'R65'], [
                ('Participation scope should match actual influence.', 'What is wrong with advertising the fictional hours consultation as a vote on all funding?', 'The promised influence exceeds the choice actually open.', ['Every consultation must control all public finance.', 'Participants can never inform service design.', 'An inaccurate invitation increases accountability.'], 'The invitation must reflect the authorised decision scope.'),
                ('Consultation and individual remedies have different purposes.', 'How should the invented consultation handle personal benefit appeals?', 'Explain that a separately verified authorised route is required.', ['Decide every appeal through a public show of hands.', 'Collect and publish private files.', 'Guarantee eligibility to all attendees.'], 'A design consultation does not confer individual adjudication powers.'),
                ('Qualitative synthesis does not estimate universal causal effects.', 'What can the participatory-budget meta-synthesis support here?', 'Contextual questions about institutions, influence and unequal participation.', ['A guaranteed effect size for every city.', 'A universal claim that one democratic form is always superior.', 'A legal mandate for the fictional council.'], 'The selected qualitative cases inform interpretation with contextual limits.'),
                ('Information sessions should be described honestly.', 'No fictional decision remains open. What is an appropriate response?', 'Describe an information session honestly or reconsider consultation.', ['Promise co-decision despite the closed choice.', 'Pretend every comment will become policy.', 'Hide the decision status.'], 'A process should not promise influence that cannot occur.'),
                ('Feedback promises require capacity.', 'What supports a credible fictional feedback commitment?', 'A responsible role and resources to examine input and explain the decision.', ['Only a large attendance target.', 'An automatic claim that every suggestion is accepted.', 'No record of the decision.'], 'A commitment needs an operating response process.')]),
        m('Choose inclusive methods and manage contributions', 'Match participation methods to a purpose while recognising missing perspectives.', [
            'Choose a participation method because it helps answer the defined question, not because it is fashionable or easy to count. A short written contribution, a facilitated discussion and a structured options exercise create different opportunities and burdens. OECD guidance asks organisers to consider participants, appropriate methods, digital tools, resources and accessibility. For the fictional service-hours question, explain what information each method can produce and which perspectives may still be missing. One popular channel rarely establishes that every intended user can contribute or that those who attend represent everyone else. [S53]',
            'Describe access barriers through tasks and circumstances rather than stereotypes. An evening-only meeting may be difficult for a person with caring responsibilities; an online-only form may exclude a supplied low-connectivity route. Offer an alternative only when a responsible team can process it and incorporate it into the same decision record. Explain how plain language, readable formats, appropriate timing and a clear contribution route could address identified barriers. Do not ask learners to recruit vulnerable people, gather personal histories or infer that a demographic category determines a person\'s abilities or preferences. [S53]',
            'Power differences can shape a discussion even when formal access is open. The participatory-budget meta-synthesis highlights context, institutionalisation and elite influence across selected qualitative cases. In an invented meeting, a dominant speaker or an established organisation may receive more attention than a less familiar perspective. Record the imbalance as a process observation, not proof of universal capture. A facilitator can make space for different contributions, clarify the question and record disagreement; the learner should not claim neutrality has been guaranteed or that a balanced speaking order resolves every structural inequality. [R65]',
            'Keep an original contribution summary that separates the issue raised, the reason supplied, the relevant decision scope and any unresolved disagreement. This is a fictional analytical record, not a personal attendance register. Avoid counting repeated messages as independent proof that a position represents the whole population. Explain the limits of the method and what an authorised organiser would need to check before making a representation claim. A good summary can preserve minority concerns and contradictory reasons without promising that every suggestion will be adopted or that a single event has completed inclusion. [S53]'],
            'A fictional online poll receives many repeated submissions from one organised group. A small evening meeting excludes an invented caring schedule, and its minutes omit a minority concern about access.',
            'Compare two participation methods and their limits. Produce a contribution summary preserving the minority concern without claiming population representativeness.',
            'Repeated submissions and attendance are not a representative population sample. Match methods to purpose, acknowledge missing perspectives and retain relevant disagreement.', ['S53', 'R65'], [
                ('Methods should fit the question and resources.', 'Which reason best supports a method choice for fictional service hours?', 'It can obtain relevant reasons accessibly within the available capacity.', ['It produces the largest number regardless of the question.', 'It is fashionable even if nobody can process input.', 'It guarantees consensus automatically.'], 'Purpose, accessibility and capacity should guide the choice.'),
                ('Repeated responses do not prove population representativeness.', 'One invented group submits the same view repeatedly. What should the summary avoid?', 'Treating each submission as proof of an independent representative preference.', ['Recording the issue and its reasons.', 'Explaining the method limit.', 'Retaining a relevant minority concern.'], 'Volume and representativeness are different claims.'),
                ('Access alternatives must connect to the same review process.', 'What makes an alternative to the fictional online poll useful?', 'Its contributions are accessible and reviewed within the decision process.', ['It collects views that nobody will read.', 'It requires private case files.', 'It silently changes the consultation question.'], 'An alternative channel needs a functioning route into analysis and response.'),
                ('Process imbalance is not proof of universal capture.', 'A dominant speaker receives most time in the invented meeting. What is proportionate?', 'Record the imbalance and propose a fairer opportunity to contribute.', ['Declare every participant corrupt.', 'Claim every consultation is captured.', 'Erase all dissent from the summary.'], 'The observation supports a bounded process correction.'),
                ('A contribution summary should preserve relevant disagreement.', 'Which fictional summary is strongest?', 'Issues, reasons, scope and unresolved disagreement with method limits.', ['Only the most popular slogan.', 'Names and sensitive histories of real users.', 'A claim that attendance proves universal agreement.'], 'A reasoned summary supports review without manufacturing consensus.')]),
        m('Close the feedback loop and evaluate the process', 'Explain how contributions affected a decision and distinguish process quality from policy outcomes.', [
            'A consultation is incomplete as an accountability process when people give views but cannot find out what happened next. OECD guidance includes feedback, explanation and evaluation of participation. A useful fictional response identifies the decision, the relevant contributions, the reasons for accepting or declining proposals and the role responsible for implementation. Explain any change in scope or timetable without inventing a legal deadline. Acknowledging receipt is only one step; it does not establish that contributions were examined or that the final choice addressed the issue participants were invited to consider. [S53]',
            'The response should represent disagreement accurately. An accepted suggestion may still depend on a budget or staffing decision held elsewhere, and a declined suggestion deserves a reason within the stated remit. Do not promise unanimity or describe an unresolved minority concern as consent. The participatory-budget synthesis shows why institutional conditions and power matter when interpreting a process. Its selected qualitative cases do not prove that feedback alone creates trust or better services, so keep the claim to what the fictional decision record actually demonstrates and identify the relevant remaining uncertainty. [R65]',
            'Evaluate process quality separately from the final public outcome. A process check can examine whether accessible routes operated, contributions were reviewed and reasons were communicated. A later outcome question asks whether the resulting service arrangement improved practical access. A portal or public report may make information visible, but the Nigerian digital-budget case offers propositions about mechanisms rather than a measured guarantee of value. Use it to question who can act on feedback and how a claimed change would be evidenced, not to declare that publication itself caused an improvement. [R64]',
            'Finish with a limited improvement proposal. Identify a process gap, a responsible role, a feasible correction and an observation for the next review. Use invented aggregate counts with a clear denominator where appropriate, and explain why they cannot establish universal representation or causation. Keep the original purpose and decision record available so a later reviewer can see what changed. The learner output should demonstrate honest influence and accountable explanation; it is not an authorisation to speak for every resident or a certificate that the institution has achieved perfect participation. [S53]'],
            'A fictional consultation receives 50 contributions and issues only a thank-you message. Later hours change because of a separate staffing decision, while publicity credits the consultation with universal access gains.',
            'Draft a reasoned feedback note, distinguish a process indicator from an outcome question and correct the unsupported attribution claim.',
            'Explain how input was considered and what remains dependent on another decision. A contribution count and a thank-you message prove neither meaningful influence nor universal access.', ['S53', 'R65', 'R64'], [
                ('Acknowledgement differs from reasoned feedback.', 'What is missing from the fictional thank-you message?', 'An explanation of consideration, decision and reasons.', ['A guarantee every suggestion was adopted.', 'Private identities of all contributors.', 'A claim that no disagreement existed.'], 'Receipt alone does not close the decision feedback loop.'),
                ('Declined contributions need an honest explanation.', 'How should a relevant minority proposal be recorded when declined?', 'State the proposal, decision reason and unresolved concern accurately.', ['Describe silence as agreement.', 'Delete it because fewer people supported it.', 'Promise an unavailable legal remedy.'], 'Accountable feedback preserves relevant disagreement.'),
                ('Process quality and policy outcomes are distinct.', 'Which is a fictional consultation process indicator?', 'The proportion of contributions receiving a reasoned disposition.', ['The proven long-term wellbeing effect of the service.', 'Universal access for every resident.', 'A guaranteed reduction in all public costs.'], 'A disposition record concerns the process, not a final causal outcome.'),
                ('Concurrent decisions constrain attribution.', 'Hours change after a separate staffing decision. What can publicity responsibly say?', 'Describe the change and the documented role of input with attribution limits.', ['Credit consultation with every access gain automatically.', 'Ignore the staffing decision.', 'Claim that a thank-you message caused the change.'], 'The observed timing alone does not establish a sole cause.'),
                ('Improvement proposals should be reviewable.', 'Which proposal addresses the fictional feedback gap?', 'Assign an authorised response owner and review documented explanations next period.', ['Increase publicity without examining input.', 'Collect real private case files.', 'Declare the process perfect after one event.'], 'A specific role, correction and observation make follow-up possible.')])])
